course: everyday
lesson: 432
title: «It was HIM!»: siblings blaming each other
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The biscuit tin in Derby was empty. «It was HIM!» said Lily. «It was HER!» said Jack. Both had chocolate on their faces. «You're both mardy and both guilty,» said Mum — «mardy» is Midlands for grumpy and sulky. No pudding for either, and both were sent to wash their faces, which made everyone laugh. When everyone is guilty, the first to own up gets the best deal.
explain: Brothers and sisters blame each other all the time, and the evidence usually says who did it. Aisha drew on the wall and wrote «OMAR IS STINKY» next to it, to make it look like her brother's work — but Omar would never write that. Dan said his little brother had been on the iPad «for ages»; the screen-time page showed Dan had it more. Sophie's big sister borrowed her new top, got ketchup on it and said «Wasn't me»; Mum sniffed it — ketchup and the sister's perfume. Busted.
explain: The worst sibling lies get someone punished for nothing. Josh in Pittsburgh told Mom his brother had called him a bad name, so his brother would lose phone time and Josh would get the TV. Dad had been in the next room: «Nobody said nothin'.» Josh lost the TV for the day. Leo broke his little sister's Lego castle and told six-year-old Eve she'd done it in her sleep; she believed him for a whole day. Tricking someone smaller is easy — that is exactly why it is unfair. He spent all Saturday rebuilding it with her.
explain: Some deals go wrong. Ruby in Edinburgh cried until her big brother Euan agreed to take the blame for a cup, and promised to do his chores for a week. She did them for one day. Euan told Mum on day two, and Ruby was in trouble for the cup AND the broken deal. Twins Mia and Maya in Toronto swapped hoodies to confuse the teacher. «Both of you can stay in at recess then, eh? Problem solved.» They didn't do it again.
explain: Honesty can even make it cheaper. Two brothers in Melbourne broke a vase wrestling. «Righto,» said Mum, «you're both gonna pay half, unless one of youse can tell me the real story.» («Youse» is Australian and Irish for «you lot».) «I pushed first,» said the younger. «But I pushed harder,» said the older. Mum let them each pay a bit less. And Noah's little sister said «Noah hit me!» every day when he hadn't — so the one day he really pushed her, Mum didn't believe her, until Noah admitted the push himself. That is the boy who cried wolf, at home.
fact: When siblings blame each other, clues like chocolate on faces, handwriting or screen-time records usually show the truth.
fact: When everyone is guilty, the first to own up usually gets the best deal.
fact: Getting a brother or sister punished for something they didn't do is one of the worst lies, because the unfair punishment is on you.
fact: Tricking a younger sibling is easy, which is exactly why it is unfair; big kids should protect little ones.
fact: Letting someone else take your blame is cowardly, and buying it with a promise you break is a second lie.
fact: Tricks that confuse grown-ups about who did it often end with everyone sharing the punishment.
fact: Exaggerating, like saying «for ages» when it was a short time, is still lying.
fact: If you borrow something and damage it, saying so at once is easier to forgive than hiding it.
fact: When both are to blame, telling the real story is fairer and often cheaper.
fact: Someone who complains falsely every day stops being believed, even when it is true — like the boy who cried wolf.
fact: «Mardy» (Midlands and North England) means grumpy and sulky; «youse» (Australia, Ireland) means «you lot»; «righto» means «all right».
exercise: Lily and Jack each blamed the other. What gave them both away? | Chocolate on their faces | A camera hidden in the kitchen | Crumbs all over their beds | Their dad saw them through the window
exercise: What does «mardy» mean? | Grumpy and sulky | Brave and strong | Funny and loud | Hungry and tired
exercise: What did Leo tell Eve about her broken Lego castle? | That she'd broken it in her sleep | That the cat had jumped up and knocked it down | That it fell down all by itself in the night | That Mum had broken it while hoovering
exercise: Why was Josh's lie about his brother so bad? | He wanted his brother punished for nothing | It was only a small fib, which is never bad | His brother really did say it | He was trying to protect his brother from Dad
exercise: Who showed that Josh's story was false? | Dad, who had been in the next room | His brother's best friend from next door | His teacher, who rang that evening | The TV remote's recording of the show
exercise: How did «OMAR IS STINKY» give Aisha away? | Omar would never write that about himself | It was written in Omar's colour | Omar was at school that day | Aisha signed her name too
exercise: What did Ruby promise Euan for taking the blame? | To do his chores for a week | To give him all her pocket money | To buy him a new cup with her savings | To tidy her own room every day
exercise: How did Ruby's deal end? | She did one day, and Euan told Mum | She did all his chores for the whole week | Euan forgot all about it | Mum never found out about the cup
exercise: Why did the twins swap hoodies? | So the teacher couldn't tell who'd been talking | Because they were both cold in the classroom | For a dress-up day at school | Because Mum told them to share their clothes
exercise: How did Mum check Dan's «for ages» story? | The iPad's screen-time page | She asked the neighbours over the fence | She timed them both the next day | She asked their teacher at school
exercise: What did Mum in Melbourne offer the brothers? | Pay a bit less if they told the real story | Pay nothing at all if they blamed the dog | Pay double if they lied | A new vase if they stopped fighting about it
exercise: How did Mum find out who wore Sophie's top? | It smelled of ketchup and the sister's perfume | The sister had written her name inside the collar | Sophie saw her on the bus | The top was in the sister's bag
exercise: Why didn't Mum believe Noah's sister the day he really pushed her? | She said «Noah hit me!» every day when he hadn't | Mum saw it was only an accident | Noah said sorry first | Mum was out at the shops when it happened and saw nothing
exercise: What did Noah do later? | Admitted the push himself | Blamed his sister again | Said nothing ever | Pushed her again
exercise: What does «youse» mean? | You lot, more than one person | Just yourself, only one person | Yes please | Your house
test: Mum in Derby: «you're both mardy and both guilty». What is she saying? | They're both sulky and both did it | They're both happy and both innocent | Only one of them did it | They're both hungry and tired
test: Big brother: «just tell your little sister she did it in her sleep, she'll believe anything». Why is this wrong? | Tricking someone smaller is easy, which makes it unfair | Little kids never believe any stories | Breaking things in your sleep is very common for little kids | It's fine as long as she's under seven
test: «it was HIM» / «no it was HER» — both have chocolate faces. What's the smartest move? | Own up first for the best deal | Keep blaming each other louder | Wash your face quickly and keep lying | Blame the dog for the chocolate instead
test: Twins swap hoodies. Teacher: «Both stay in at recess then, eh?» Why does the trick backfire? | Confusing who did it makes both share the punishment | The teacher liked their hoodies | Recess was cancelled for the whole class anyway because of rain | They get extra recess
test: Dan: «he's been on it FOREVER». The screen shows Dan had it longer. What kind of lie is this? | An exaggeration, which is still lying | Not a lie, only a feeling | The truth, because it felt long to Dan | A joke that everyone enjoys
test: What would have happened if Sophie's sister had said at once «sorry, I spilled ketchup on your top»? | Sophie would likely forgive a stain more easily | Sophie would never speak to her again | The top would wash clean by itself | Mum would ground her for a whole year for the stain
test: Ruby got Euan to take the blame and then broke the deal. How many wrongs is that? | Two: the blame and the broken promise | None, because he agreed to it | One: only the cup | Three: the cup, the tears and the chores
test: Little sister: «Noah hit me!» — every day, never true. Which story is this like? | The boy who cried wolf | Goldilocks and the three bears | Jack and the beanstalk | The tortoise and the hare
test: Mum in Melbourne: «Righto, youse are both paying half.» One says «I pushed first», the other «I pushed harder». What did honesty get them? | They each paid a bit less | They had to pay double | They got a new vase for free | Nothing at all, it made no difference
test: Josh's dad: «Nobody said nothin'.» What does Dad mean? | Nobody said anything bad | Somebody did say something | Josh should say nothing | He wasn't listening
test: In a sitcom, a kid frames his brother and everyone laughs at the brother's punishment. What is true in real life? | Getting someone punished for nothing is a cruel lie | It's always just a harmless joke between brothers | The brother deserves it anyway | Parents never find out who really did it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which causing a sibling to be punished for an act he did not commit is regarded as a grave falsehood. | The unjust punishment is borne by an innocent person | Siblings are entitled to lie to one another | It improves the subsequent behaviour of the sibling so punished | It is of no consequence within the family
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who complains falsely and repeatedly of being struck. | Her complaints cease to be believed, even when genuine | Her complaints are believed with greater readiness | Repeated complaints compel belief | False complaints have no effect upon trust
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an arrangement whereby one sibling assumes the blame in exchange for a promise subsequently broken? | It comprises both a cowardly evasion and a further falsehood | It is a fair and lawful bargain | It removes all responsibility from the guilty sibling in every respect | It is honest, the elder sibling having consented
exam: It is given that two siblings jointly responsible for breaking a vase each give a truthful account. Determine the outcome reported. | Each was required to pay a reduced share | Each was required to pay the whole cost | Neither was required to pay anything | Both were punished more severely for the delay
